;; The run cases whose expected outcome is derived from the test's own source by hand (n-c-last, round 54; test/c/selfref.ae):
;; programs with no expected-output or status file of their own (mir's lacc and new programs with neither .expectrc nor .expect,
;; cxgo's two), each read and its outcome worked out from C23 and Arbace's target (x86-64 psABI, musl 1.2.6: puts returns 0 on
;; success, src/stdio/puts.c; printf's %p is 0x and the hex digits; %f of a NaN or an infinity "nan" and "inf"), never from a
;; compiler's run. Most of lacc's programs as MIR keeps them check themselves (main returns (unsigned char)(EXPR) != VALUE, 0 when
;; right). :stdout is the exact output, or :any where C leaves it unspecified (then the status alone is the verdict). Left with gcc's
;; run, as no outcome follows from the source alone: mir/lacc/union-bitfield.c (a union initialized by its 13-bit first member read
;; through its unsigned long: the other bits unspecified), mir/lacc/stringify.c (its 139 bytes of stringified tokens not worked out
;; here), mir/new/setjmp2.c (setjmp called through a pointer: undefined, C23 7.13.1.1), mir/new/non-pedantic-macro-redef.c (a macro
;; redefined differently: a constraint violation, its meaning gcc's extension); mir/lacc/line-directive.c stays
;; test/c/selfref-exceptions.edn's (its .expectrc counts the file's name as mir's runner passes it).
;; Read with the Clojure reader.
